WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.891
In 1912 the largest and most luxurious ship in the world set sail on its maiden voyage from England to America.

2
00:00:07.892 --> 00:00:15.115
When it left port on April 10, with over 2,200 people on board, the Titanic was believed to be unsinkable.

3
00:00:15.116 --> 00:00:20.827
But on April 15, the ship sank in the Atlantic Ocean after hitting an iceberg.

4
00:00:20.828 --> 00:00:29.132
Because there were not enough lifeboats for everyone, around 1,500 people—rich and poor alike—perished in the wreck.

5
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:33.001
The story of the Titanic shocked the world when the great ship sank.

6
00:00:33.002 --> 00:00:39.008
After the sinking, many people wanted to find the ship, and some even wanted to raise it.

7
00:00:39.009 --> 00:00:43.448
But this was difficult, if not impossible, with the technology at the time.

8
00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:50.231
In the late 1970s and early 1980s, several serious expeditions tried to locate the ship.

9
00:00:50.232 --> 00:00:54.663
Using sonar, crew members scanned large areas of the seafloor.

10
00:00:54.664 --> 00:00:57.371
None of these efforts were successful.

11
00:00:57.372 --> 00:01:02.015
U.S. Navy researcher Robert Ballard was fascinated by the Titanic story.

12
00:01:02.016 --> 00:01:07.347
Even after a failed effort to locate the ship in 1977, he didn't give up. In

13
00:01:07.348 --> 00:01:11.772
the summer of 1985, Ballard worked with a French team. After

14
00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:17.557
scanning the seafloor with high-resolution sonar for five weeks, the search had turned up nothing. Ballard

15
00:01:17.558 --> 00:01:22.030
continued the search using a new deep-sea camera device instead. For

16
00:01:22.031 --> 00:01:27.272
nine more days, crew members saw nothing but the seafloor on the research vessel's screens. But

17
00:01:27.273 --> 00:01:32.882
just after midnight on September 1, they began to see unusual objects on the seafloor. Sure

18
00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:36.325
enough, the objects were debris from the Titanic .

19
00:01:36.326 --> 00:01:39.726
Soon researchers saw images of the shipwreck itself.

20
00:01:39.727 --> 00:01:42.152
The Titanic had been found!

21
00:01:42.153 --> 00:01:45.533
News of Ballard's discovery traveled around the world.

22
00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:49.935
People were eager to learn more about what happened to the ship in 1912.

23
00:01:49.936 --> 00:01:53.965
Some survivors had said that the unsinkable ship had broken apart.

24
00:01:53.966 --> 00:01:58.408
But most people assumed that the ship sank in one piece.

25
00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:03.785
This mystery was finally solved: the ship was found in two pieces.

26
00:02:03.786 --> 00:02:07.012
The following summer Ballard returned to the wreck.

27
00:02:07.013 --> 00:02:12.183
This time he went down 3.8 kilometers in a deep-sea submarine to see the wreck in person.

28
00:02:12.184 --> 00:02:15.133
The ship was rusted but well preserved.

29
00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:18.825
Some objects inside the ship remained where their owners had left them.

30
00:02:18.826 --> 00:02:24.155
Ballard explored the ship's bow and stern sections, as well as the debris field between them.

31
00:02:24.156 --> 00:02:26.952
He was careful not to disturb anything.

32
00:02:26.953 --> 00:02:34.381
Since so many people had died in the disaster, he thought it would be disrespectful to take objects from the site.

33
00:02:34.382 --> 00:02:38.205
After Ballard's discovery many others explored the wreck.

34
00:02:38.206 --> 00:02:44.547
Not only did these scientists learn more about the ship, they also made surprising discoveries about the ocean.

35
00:02:44.548 --> 00:02:48.220
New species of bacteria were discovered in the rust on the ship.

36
00:02:48.221 --> 00:02:51.961
Scientists had expected the ocean floor to be a barren desert.

37
00:02:51.962 --> 00:02:56.715
Instead they saw that it was home to a variety of life.

38
00:02:56.716 --> 00:03:03.084
Controversially a French expedition recovered over 1,800 objects from the wreck in 1987.

39
00:03:03.085 --> 00:03:07.565
Ballard immediately spoke out about this, believing the wreck to be a grave site. The

40
00:03:07.566 --> 00:03:11.100
remaining survivors agreed with him, but not everyone did. Future

41
00:03:11.101 --> 00:03:14.782
expeditions would recover even more objects. Many

42
00:03:14.783 --> 00:03:23.003
of these objects were quite ordinary: plates, forks, cups, glasses, decorations, and passengers' belongings. Other

43
00:03:23.004 --> 00:03:25.515
objects have been more surprising. Paper

44
00:03:25.516 --> 00:03:29.092
and clothing survived for decades on the ocean floor. Bottles

45
00:03:29.093 --> 00:03:35.028
of wine, jars of olives, and vials of perfume still seemed as good as new. Clocks

46
00:03:35.029 --> 00:03:40.509
and watches still showed times around 2:20 a.m.—the time the ship sank. The

47
00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.544
ship's artifacts have been displayed in exhibitions around the world. Supporters

48
00:03:44.545 --> 00:03:50.838
of recovering artifacts argue that recovering and preserving the objects helps preserve history. This

49
00:03:50.839 --> 00:03:54.504
may no longer be a debate for the Titanic 's contents.

50
00:03:54.505 --> 00:03:58.839
Scientists have learned that the wreck itself is rapidly deteriorating.

51
00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:02.964
Luckily, the sad story of the Titanic is far from being lost.

52
00:04:02.965 --> 00:04:11.989
Countless books, movies, new discoveries, and exhibitions continue to tell the story of the famous ship's first, and only, voyage.

